==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=ring of muscle around body openings|lbor|la-lat>sphinctēr>sphincter|tree=1}}
{{lbor|en|la-lat|sphinctēr||the muscle of the anus}}, from {{der|en|grc|σφῐγκτήρ||lace, band; contractile muscle}}, ultimately of {{der|en|qsb-grc}} origin. Possibly related to {{m|en|sphinx||the strangler}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsfɪŋk.tɚ/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sphincter.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|muscle}} A [[ringlike]] [[band]] of [[muscle]] that surrounds a [[bodily]] [[opening]] (such as the [[anus]] or the openings of the [[stomach]]), [[constrict]]ing and [[relax]]ing as required for normal [[physiological]] functioning.
#: {{hypo|en|anal sphincter|lissosphincter|lower esophageal sphincter|pyloric sphincter|rhabdosphincter|sphincter of Oddi|upper esophageal sphincter|urethral sphincter}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''sphincter''' of the bladder}}
#: {{ux|en|the iris '''sphincter''' in the eye}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1982|author=w:Lawrence Durrell|title=[[w:Constance (novel)|Constance]]|series=w:The Avignon Quintet|publisher=w:Faber & Faber|page=836|text=She decided that she would force him to climax first by the sheer strength of her young animal control, the strength of her '''sphincters''' {{...}}}}

